WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
SPEAKER 1: The
Founders understood

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:05.100
that slavery was wrong
on principle, that it's,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.720
by definition, an injustice.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:09.924
And they understood it as such.

00:00:09.924 --> 00:00:13.242
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.080
Hamilton and Madison both
referred to slaves as men.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.360
This is a critical insight,
because if slaves are men,

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.490
if slaves are human
beings, then that

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:27.700
means that they have
inalienable natural rights.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.220
And that means that
slavery can only

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.830
rest on the basis
of positive law,

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.870
that it's, by
definition, an injustice.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.990
And they understood it as such.

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:41.200
Thomas Jefferson, in one of
the most famous statements

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.360
on slavery to come out
of the American Founding,

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.390
query 18 in "Notes on
the State of Virginia,"

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:50.230
explains that slavery is
at war with human nature

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:51.460
and with justice.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.970
He says it's bad for
slaves because it teaches

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:57.940
them to hate their country,
and it teaches them

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:59.800
to lack self-assertion.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.230
But it's also bad for masters.

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.959
It teaches masters
to become tyrants.

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:05.950
It warps them.

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:08.350
It teaches them to have
contempt for the rights

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:09.640
of other human beings.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.320
And once they learn
to have contempt

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.780
for the rights of
their slaves, they then

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.820
transfer that and
learn to have contempt

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:19.030
for the rights of others
who are not slaves.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.430
Finally, it's bad for society.

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.770
It's bad for the
country as a whole.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.950
Jefferson says that we are
so wrong on the question

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.460
of slavery that it is certain
that God will punish us

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:31.960
if we don't get it right.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.580
In fact, he says if
it ever came down

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:37.510
to a contest between
slaves and masters

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.160
over the future of the
country, Jefferson says,

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:43.720
quote, "The Almighty
has no attribute which

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.080
can take side with us in
such a contest," unquote.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.940
So, in short, we can see pretty
quickly that the Founding

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:55.240
Fathers understood that
they had a colossal problem

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.390
on their hands, that slavery
was a massive injustice.

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.790
And they understood it as such.

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:03.730
What did the Founders
do about slavery?

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:07.330
Because again, to say that
they did not end slavery

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:10.449
altogether is not
the same as saying

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:12.350
that they did nothing about it.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.490
For instance,
during the founding

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:19.990
and early American period,
nine states became free states.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:23.690
All of the states, the original
states north of Maryland--

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.470
so from Pennsylvania
northward-- became free states,

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.680
abolished slavery in
one way or another.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.500
Slave states eased
their manumission laws.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:36.160
Now, a manumission is simply an
individual act of emancipation

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:39.760
where an individual owner
frees an individual slave.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:42.850
So it was made easier
to free one's slaves

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.020
in the Southern states
that retain slavery.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.550
And as a result, thousands
of slaves were manumitted.

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:51.520
Now, this does not end
the institution of slavery

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:52.150
altogether.

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.730
But it is certainly
important to you

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.340
if you are one of those
thousands who was manumitted

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.030
during this period.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:03.670
We also see instances of
Southern courts favoring

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:07.030
the claims of slaves who
are suing for their freedom.

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:12.130
In fact, this is how the
Dred Scott case got its start

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:14.020
in the Missouri State courts.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:17.350
In ruling against
Dred Scott in 1850,

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:19.450
the Missouri Supreme
Court admitted

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.350
that if Dred Scott's case
had come before the court,

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:27.100
say, 10 years earlier, they most
certainly would have freed him.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.830
But times had changed,
and so they were no longer

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.880
honoring the friendly agreement
that they had with free states

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.340
to respect the laws of
those states because

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.100
of the intensification of
the slavery controversy.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:42.220
There were also reforms
in the slave states

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.040
that tended to recognize
the humanity of slaves.

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.200
By the beginning of
the 19th century,

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:51.400
North Carolina had reformed its
laws so that killing a slave

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:54.430
carried the same penalty
as killing a free person.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:56.650
It was still hard to
obtain a conviction,

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.630
but at least this
is a recognition

00:03:58.630 --> 00:04:01.150
that a slave is, in fact,
a human being and not

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.980
an article of livestock.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:06.580
There was the abolition of the
slave trade, which was legally

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:10.960
abolished in 1807, and the
abolition took effect in 1888.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.330
Later on, the slave
trade was declared

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:16.570
to be piracy, making it
a death penalty offense.

00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:18.850
This was a law
which was extremely

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:20.865
hard to enforce in
the age of sale,

00:04:20.865 --> 00:04:22.990
but at least you have a
declaration of a commitment

00:04:22.990 --> 00:04:25.630
to end this inhumane practice.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.330
If one looks to the
Constitution itself,

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:31.510
one notices that
conspicuously absent

00:04:31.510 --> 00:04:34.450
from the entire Constitution,
including the provisions that

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:37.630
deal directly with slavery,
is the word "slave"

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.460
in any variation.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:42.730
Madison, Frederick Douglass,
Abraham Lincoln all

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:45.730
came to the same conclusion
about why this was the case.

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:49.780
They did not want to taint the
document with any implication

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.030
that slavery existed, so
that if someone came along

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.310
in the future without
any particular knowledge

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:56.710
of our history or
politics, they could not

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.740
read the original Constitution
and know that slavery

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:01.180
had been a part of it.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:05.140
If one turns to the particular
provisions in the Constitution

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.540
that deal with
slavery, we also can

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.680
see ambiguities and
compromises that

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.800
reflect the Founders' desire to
seize slavery gradually removed

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.300
from our society.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.990
The Importation Clause
in Article I Section 9

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:25.110
was looked upon by many
people as a death sentence

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.050
for slavery.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.020
It was widely
believed at the time

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.050
that slave populations could
not be self-sustaining,

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:35.760
that only continual importation
would allow slavery to survive

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.180
in a particular community.

00:05:37.180 --> 00:05:39.180
And so many people,
such as James Wilson,

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.460
believed that cutting
off the slave trade

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.740
killed slavery itself.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:47.460
And that proved to be
largely true in many places,

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:48.810
except in America.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:50.790
In America, the
slave population,

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.790
contrary to expectations,
proved to be self-supporting.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.610
Finally, if one is looking
at what the Founders did

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:59.670
about slavery, perhaps
the most important thing

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:03.150
that they did about slavery was
to pass a piece of legislation

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.830
called the Northwest Ordinance.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:09.480
So the Northwest territory
was the full area

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.910
in which the federal government
could control slavery,

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.240
and the Northwest Ordinance
banned it in perpetuity.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:18.330
And it was passed by a
large margin in Congress

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:21.630
both times under both
forms of government.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:24.060
This was significant
in a number of ways.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.960
Indiana, when it petitioned for
statehood, asked for the right

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.120
to be exempt from the
Northwest Ordinance.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.460
Now, one does not ask
for that exemption

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.080
if one wants to be a free state.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.580
And the federal
government told them

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:36.750
that they were not exempted.

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:38.958
They could remain free under
the Northwest Ordinance,

00:06:38.958 --> 00:06:41.620
or they could
become a free state.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:44.370
So it certainly
made one state that

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:46.540
might have been a slave
state into a free state.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:50.670
It provided, when we got to the
Civil War, an enormous amount

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.130
of manpower and
material resources

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.760
for the Union war effort.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:58.800
And finally, the language
of the slavery prohibition

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.710
in the Northwest
Ordinance became the basis

00:07:01.710 --> 00:07:05.580
for the 13th Amendment to the
Constitution outlawing slavery

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:09.210
nationwide by
constitutional amendment.

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:12.390
So many things were done
to restrict slavery--

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:14.730
to hem it in, as
Lincoln would later

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:19.430
say-- within the narrowest
limits of necessity.

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:20.880
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you for watching.

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:22.850
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlights series

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:25.760
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:26.960
online courses.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.090
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

